00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.840
In this tutorial we'll take a look at Depth&nbsp;
Buffers in OpenGL and also see how we can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.200
use of them for a little graphical effect.
You might remember that we've already made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.440
use of the Depth Buffer in the "Going 3D"&nbsp;
tutorial in order to fix a weird issue we had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.000
Since the buffer is turned off by default,&nbsp;
we want to make sure we have it enabled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.680
and that we clear it each frame just like the&nbsp;
Color Buffer. Now what this buffer basically does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.760
is that it stores "depth" values that represent&nbsp;
how far away from the near plane of the projection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.400
matrix a certain fragment is. A depth value of&nbsp;
0 meaning that it's right on the near plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.560
and of 1 meaning it's on the far plane.
Using this depth information, we can asses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.880
which object should be in front of which other&nbsp;
object. We can do this by using the glDepthFunc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:35.040
and inserting one of the following inputs. By&nbsp;
default OpenGL chooses GL_LESS which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.360
if the depth value of an object is less than that&nbsp;
of the current depth value, then the first value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.880
replaces the second. In most circumstances&nbsp;
you should use GL_LESS, but I suppose you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.560
could also choose one of the others if you want&nbsp;
your game or application to be mind bending.
&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.840
Now the cool part. Let's visualize the depth&nbsp;
buffer. We can easily do this by going to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:06.160
fragment shader and outputting gl_FragCoord.z as&nbsp;
the FragColor. The problem is, that as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.920
we press run, you'll notice the screen is mostly&nbsp;
pure white. The only way to see a bit of darkness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:18.160
is to get very close to an object. This is due&nbsp;
to the fact that depth in OpenGL is not linear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.920
If the depth was linear, then we would have&nbsp;
the same amount of precision for depth at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.000
close distance as we would at a far away&nbsp;
distance. Since we almost always focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.800
things that are close to us, we want to make the&nbsp;
precision be very high near us, and low away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.240
us. This is achieved by using this formula.&nbsp;
Don't worry, we don't have to implement it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.680
since OpenGL does it automatically.
Sometimes though, you want to use another formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:53.680
so in order to do that, we must first get the&nbsp;
z-value by linearizing the depth function, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.440
can be done using this function. Now using this&nbsp;
function we get the z value which keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.960
is not normalized. This value is simply the&nbsp;
distance from the near plane. So let's declare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.960
the near and far constants of our frustum, and&nbsp;
divide the linear depth by the far length to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:22.160
quickly normalize it just to see what the results&nbsp;
look like. Now let's just look at a quick problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.720
you can get, and then I'll show you the cool&nbsp;
effect we can achieve with depth buffers.
&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.880
So the main issue that arises from depth buffers&nbsp;
is called Z-fighting, and it occurs because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.920
two or more triangles have the same depth&nbsp;
buffer and thus the depth function can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.520
decide which one is closer than the other, and&nbsp;
thus it keeps changing between them constantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.320
An easy fix to this is usually to make sure you&nbsp;
don't have triangles that are too close to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:54.400
another and parallel. If Z-fighting appears at a&nbsp;
far away distance, then you might also consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.600
tweaking the function of the depth buffer so&nbsp;
that you have more precision at that distance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.920
And the final trick is to use a bigger integer&nbsp;
for the depth buffer. Usually by default it uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.320
24bits, but you could change it to 32bits if your&nbsp;
card supports it. This will increase the precision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.920
and thus decrease the change of Z-fighting.
Now for the cool effect. We can take the z value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.800
and plug it into a logistic function for&nbsp;
which we have a steepness parameter, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.120
an offset parameter. The steepness will control&nbsp;
how fast the depth value changes from close to 0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.360
to close to 1, and the offset will determine&nbsp;
at what z-value this change is half way done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.400
The cool thing about this, is that this can give&nbsp;
you smooth edges for the end of your frustum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.240
if you want that, but the even nicer thing, is&nbsp;
that this can somewhat simulate a fog effect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.920
So let's change our background color to a grey,&nbsp;
and calculate the depth value using our function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.320
Then multiply our usual direcLight() by the&nbsp;
reverse of our depth value, and also add the depth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.960
value times the grey color from before. Now we&nbsp;
should have a nice simple fog effect. Keep in mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.840
that you might need to adjust the steepness&nbsp;
and offset a bit to get it just right.
&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.920
Have fun playing around&nbsp;
with different effects!
