00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.360
In this tutorial we'll take a look at the&nbsp;
Stencil Buffer and how we can use it to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.680
useful effects such as outlining a model.
So the Stencil Buffer, just like the Depth Buffer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:34.320
holds a value for each pixel you can see, these&nbsp;
values being used for image masking in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.080
Unlike the Depth Buffer though,&nbsp;
where each pixel holds between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.480
2 and 4 bytes of data, for the Stencil&nbsp;
Buffer each pixel only holds 1 byte of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:52.240
so values from 0 to 255. But you'll&nbsp;
mainly only use the values of 0 and 1.
&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.200
So let's look at how we can work with this&nbsp;
new buffer. First of all we have the function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.640
glStencilMask that allows us to choose which&nbsp;
parts of the Stencil Buffer we want to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.960
to modify. It simply takes a pixel from the mask,&nbsp;
and a corresponding pixel from the Stencil Buffer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.760
and applies a bitwise "AND" comparison on them.&nbsp;
Keep in mind each pixel has a byte of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:22.960
so 8 bits. A bitwise "AND" operation compares&nbsp;
each bit with it's corresponding counterpart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:32.160
only outputs 1 if they are both 1. Therefore if we&nbsp;
input 0x00 into glStencilMask, which means that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:38.080
have 8 bits equal to 0, then all the comparisons&nbsp;
will fail and the Stencil Buffer won't change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:46.320
at all. But if we input 0xFF into glStencilMask,&nbsp;
then all the bits of the mask will be 1 since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:53.200
0xFF is equal to 8 ones, and so we'll be able&nbsp;
to modify any part of the Stencil Buffer.
&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:59.040
Now let's look at two more functions we can&nbsp;
make use of: glStencilFunc, and glStencilOp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.040
glStencilFunc allows us to control how the&nbsp;
Stencil Buffer passes a test or fails a test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:11.280
while glStencilOp allows us to dictate&nbsp;
what happens when the stencil test fails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.480
when the stencil test passes but the&nbsp;
depth test fails, and when both pass.
&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.920
Let's take a deeper look at glStencilFunc.&nbsp;
It takes in three arguments: a function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:28.640
a reference value, and a mask. The function can&nbsp;
be one of these, by default being set to GL_ALWAYS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.560
so the test always passes. The reference value&nbsp;
is simply the value we use to compare in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:40.480
function. Notice how before comparing the stencil&nbsp;
value with the reference value we apply a bitwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.560
AND operation to both using the mask. This means&nbsp;
that if you want to compare the numerical value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.800
of the two accurately you will want your&nbsp;
mask to be 0xFF so that nothing changes.
&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:08:01.760
Now for glStencilOp, it has four arguments: sfail,&nbsp;
dpfail, and dppass. These stand for stencil fail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.400
depth fail, and depth pass. For all of&nbsp;
these you can choose between the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.520
options. By default they all have GL_KEEP&nbsp;
which basically means that nothing changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.400
There's not really much more to say about these&nbsp;
functions, so if you want to know more about them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.240
look them up in the documentation.
The Stencil Buffer can be used for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:27.120
things such as portals, mirrors, and more, but an&nbsp;
easy feature to implement is outlining of models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.720
so let's take a look at that. We first want to&nbsp;
render our object like we normally do and update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:39.280
the stencil buffer with 1s everywhere we have&nbsp;
a fragment from our object, and 0 everywhere we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:44.720
don't have a fragment from our object. This&nbsp;
will essentially create a figure like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.240
Then we want to disable writing to the stencil&nbsp;
buffer so we don't accidentally modify it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:56.400
and also disable depth testing so that we can make&nbsp;
sure the next object we draw will be completely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.080
front of the previous one. Now we want to render&nbsp;
a scaled up version of the object we had before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.920
but this time in a flat color, and with the&nbsp;
following condition: we only draw it's fragments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:14.800
where the stencil value is not 1, so basically not&nbsp;
where the silhouette of the previous object was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.040
Then we just restore writing to the&nbsp;
stencil and enable the depth buffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.640
again. That's all we have to do.
Now let's implement it in code. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:29.360
start of by enabling our stencil buffer using&nbsp;
glEnable(GL_STENCIL_TEST), and making sure we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:36.160
also have our depth buffer enabled. Then we&nbsp;
use glStencilOp plugging in GL_KEEP, GL_KEEP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:42.320
and GL_REPLACE. This will make it so that&nbsp;
when both the depth and stencil tests pass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:47.840
we'll use the reference value specified by&nbsp;
glStencilFunc. Now let's make sure we clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.960
all the buffers before each frame, and go&nbsp;
on to the part where the magic happens.
&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:58.640
First we specify our stencil test always&nbsp;
passes, and set the reference value to 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:04.720
Then we enable writing to all of our stencil&nbsp;
buffer with a stencil mask of all 1s. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.960
we simply draw our object. By this point in time,&nbsp;
our stencil buffer looks like this, and our color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:18.640
buffer like this. Remember that all these values&nbsp;
are 1 because we specified in the glStencilOp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:25.040
function that if both the stencil and depth&nbsp;
tests pass, then make the pixel of the stencil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:31.520
buffer equal to 1. Now for the outline we want our&nbsp;
stencil test to only pass when it's not equal to 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:37.200
and the depth test passes. Then we disable out&nbsp;
depth buffer for previously mentioned reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.760
and disable writing to the stencil mask&nbsp;
so we can keep our original silhouette.
&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.640
Now since we want to make the outline a flat&nbsp;
color, we'll have to create two new shaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:52.320
and a shader program. So let's first create the&nbsp;
shader program like so. Then for "outlining.frag"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:58.240
we simply want to return a color, while for&nbsp;
"outlining.vert" we want to get the position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:04.320
and all the uniforms related to transformations,&nbsp;
plus a new float uniform called outlining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.440
which we'll multiply with the scale matrix. Now&nbsp;
back in the main function we'll want to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:16.240
that outlining uniform to the shader with a value&nbsp;
of something like 1.08. Then we simply draw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:22.400
same object again, but this time using the other&nbsp;
shaders. The last step we need to do is to enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:28.400
writing to the whole stencil, clear it by always&nbsp;
passing the test and replacing the values with 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.720
and enabling the depth buffer. Now if&nbsp;
you run the program, you should see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:38.640
an outline around your object. If your object&nbsp;
has it's origin at its geometrical center and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:44.560
if it doesn't have very complex shapes, then the&nbsp;
outline probably looks fine. In my case though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:51.040
you can see that the origin is not at the center&nbsp;
since the outline is skewed upwards, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:57.200
the shapes are pretty complex, with many curves,&nbsp;
therefore this method won't work that well.
&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.880
A better method that doesn't take much would&nbsp;
be to import the normals in the vertex shader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:08.880
and add those to the position vector, multiplying&nbsp;
them by the outlining, which we'll remove from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:16.720
scale matrix, and which we'll now lower from&nbsp;
1.08 to 0.08. Now when scaling the vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:23.280
instead of scaling them from their origin, we&nbsp;
are sort of scaling them outwardly from the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:29.760
using the normal as a reference for what "outward"&nbsp;
means. This will give you much better results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:34.720
with one exception. If you have hard&nbsp;
edges, then your normals will be close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:40.720
to perpendicular to one another and so will&nbsp;
create a little gap when expanding the model.
&nbsp;

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:45.760
Therefore a third solution to this would be to&nbsp;
simply have another model that's bigger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:52.080
the first one. To be more specific, it has to be&nbsp;
thicker, not necessarily bigger. You can achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:57.680
something like this using Blender's solidify&nbsp;
modifier. Here you can see I have the initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:04.080
model, and the bigger version of it. Now I've&nbsp;
already exported both as different models, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.920
only have to import them into my program like so.&nbsp;
Then instead of drawing the object a second time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.840
I simply draw the bigger version of it, this&nbsp;
time not even needing a uniform to scale it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:22.000
And as you can see, this gives the best results,&nbsp;
though at the cost of doubling the storage cost...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:27.200
You could of course make a function similar&nbsp;
to the solidify function that blender has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:33.840
but that would be slightly more&nbsp;
complicated for this tutorial.
&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.800
Today I'll show you what face culling&nbsp;
is, and how it affects performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:45.840
We are also gonna measure this performance&nbsp;
change by making an FPS counter.
&nbsp;

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:51.200
So face culling is a step in the graphics pipeline&nbsp;
that decides if a triangle will move on to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:57.280
fragment shader (aka, if the triangle will be&nbsp;
drawn or not). OpenGL decides this by seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:02.320
which side of the triangle is currently facing the&nbsp;
camera. Generally speaking, in most 3D graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:06.960
programs, it is the front side of a triangle&nbsp;
that is sent to the fragment shader, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:12.480
back side of a triangle that is discarded. 
The way OpenGL figures out which side is which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:18.160
is by an index convention, which can either&nbsp;
be clockwise, or counter-clockwise. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:23.680
counter-clockwise framework, if the order of the&nbsp;
indices of a triangle are counter-clockwise when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:29.680
facing us, then the side we see, is the front&nbsp;
side. Likewise, if the order of the indices of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:36.080
triangle are clockwise when facing us, then the&nbsp;
side we see, is the back side. For a clockwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:41.520
framework it's the exact opposite. Most graphics&nbsp;
programs use a counter clockwise standard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:46.640
but don't expect all of them to use this.
Now in order to put all of this into code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:51.040
we just have to enable the face culling&nbsp;
using glEnable with GL_CULL_FACE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:58.240
specify which face we want to keep, 99% of the&nbsp;
time that will be GL_FRONT, and then specify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.600
standard we want to use. Again I suggest using&nbsp;
the counter-clockwise one since from what I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:08.240
seen it's more common than the clockwise one.
Now if we run the program, you'll notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:13.360
when we get inside an object, we won't be able&nbsp;
to see its insides, since it contains the backs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:18.240
of the triangles, which get discarded.&nbsp;
Therefore we only see the background.
&nbsp;

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:23.760
So let's see if this makes any difference in&nbsp;
performance. For that we'll need an FPS counter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.400
which I'll display in the title of the window.&nbsp;
Let's start by creating three doubles for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:33.600
the previous time, the current time, and the&nbsp;
difference of these two. Then we also want an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.480
unsigned integer that will act as a counter&nbsp;
to see how many frames we have in a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:43.680
amount of time. Now, FPS, is simply the&nbsp;
amount of frames you get in a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:49.360
So that means that in order to get the FPS, we&nbsp;
can count the number of frames we get in a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:55.120
a frame being one loop in our main while loop.&nbsp;
But that would also mean that our FPS will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:01.120
updated only once a second. Instead let's&nbsp;
update it every 30th of a second for example.
&nbsp;

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:07.280
To do that we just need to get the current time&nbsp;
in seconds using glfwGetTime, the time difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:13.680
and increment the counter. Then if the difference&nbsp;
is higher or equal to a 30th of a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:19.120
we go ahead with the measurement of the FPS.&nbsp;
The FPS will simply be equal to 1 divided by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.400
time difference, which is the amount of frames&nbsp;
in a second that this time difference gives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:29.840
but the time difference contains multiple frames,&nbsp;
which are equal to the counter, so we also need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:35.600
multiply it with the counter as well. Now we could&nbsp;
stop here, but it's also useful to know how long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:41.040
a frame takes in terms of milliseconds. To do&nbsp;
that we simply divide the time difference by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:46.480
the counter which gives us the number of seconds&nbsp;
a frame takes, and then multiply that by 1000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.600
transform it into milliseconds. Then we simply&nbsp;
put together the new title and apply it to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:57.920
window using glfwSetWindowTitle. Then we want&nbsp;
to set the previous time as the current time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:02.720
in order to get the time difference back&nbsp;
to 0, and also set the counter to 0.
&nbsp;

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.880
Now if you start your program, you'll be able&nbsp;
to see the amount of frames you have. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:12.640
are stuck on 60, then that means that you have&nbsp;
VSync on, which tries to keep your FPS constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:19.600
to 60 frames per second. If you wish to disable&nbsp;
this, then write glfwSwapInterval(0) in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:25.760
main function. Keep in mind that this will only be&nbsp;
able to deactivate VSync if VSync is not forced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:31.200
your graphics driver. In any case, I recommend&nbsp;
keeping it at 60 frames per second. But if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:36.320
don't want to do that, at least make sure that the&nbsp;
functions that handle user inputs are put into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:41.920
if statement that works periodically like this&nbsp;
one, otherwise the responsiveness of your inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:47.040
will vary with your FPS, which you do not want.
And just to show you that face culling improves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:53.280
your FPS, here is a very high resolution model&nbsp;
and the difference in FPS. The difference is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:58.480
big here, but it's noticeable in the numbers.&nbsp;
When you have multiple models and a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:01.760
stuff happening, the difference will&nbsp;
become even more noticeable.
&nbsp;

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.960
In this tutorial I'll show you how to&nbsp;
quickly get transparency turned on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:14.880
and also how to make use of the&nbsp;
blending feature in OpenGL.
&nbsp;

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:21.040
So as you might have noticed, all the textures&nbsp;
we've been using so far had 4 components: RED,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:27.440
GREEN, BLUE, and ALPHA (RGBA). The first three&nbsp;
of those give color to our scene, while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:32.880
last one controls the level of transparency&nbsp;
different objects have. Now if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:38.560
this grass model I have imported you'll notice&nbsp;
there is no transparency to it. To enable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:43.760
we simply have to create a new fragment shader&nbsp;
which will be identical to our normal fragment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:50.240
shader, but we'll check if the alpha value is&nbsp;
smaller than a certain threshold, and if it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:55.200
we'll discard that fragment. Don't forget to&nbsp;
also make a new shader program for this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:00.560
fragment shader. So if you run the program, you&nbsp;
should see the grass is now as it should be.
&nbsp;

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:06.320
Now I'll just add a bunch of randomly placed&nbsp;
transparent windows. Since the code here is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:12.800
relevant to OpenGL I won't be explaining it. The&nbsp;
shader I use for these is a very basic shader that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:18.880
just displays the textures without any lighting.&nbsp;
So now as you can see there are a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:25.920
WINDOWS, but even though in my texture they&nbsp;
are see-through, here they are not. In order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:32.800
achieve a see-through effect we need blending.
Now for a bit of theory. This is the formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:38.800
OpenGL uses for blending different colors&nbsp;
together. All the C terms stand for Color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:44.320
while the T terms stand for transparency.&nbsp;
Which in case you didn't know or forgot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:52.400
an alpha level of 0 is fully transparent and an&nbsp;
alpha level of 1 is fully opaque. And then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:58.240
source color is the color in the fragment shader,&nbsp;
while the destination color is the color in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:03.920
color buffer. These transparencies can have&nbsp;
different formulas. The most common one is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:09.360
in which the source gets its transparency value&nbsp;
from the alpha part of the source color, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:16.640
the destination's transparency is 1 - the alpha&nbsp;
value of the source. So now let's tell OpenGL we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:23.360
want to use this configuration using glBlendFunc&nbsp;
specifying the source and destination functions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:28.560
Here is a list of some functions you might want&nbsp;
to use for one reason or another. Then if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:34.960
want to you can also use glBlendEquation with one&nbsp;
of these arguments in order to specify how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:40.640
want the previous colors to interact, basically&nbsp;
changing the default equation. And lastly you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:47.440
use the glBlendFuncSeparate function to choose how&nbsp;
to interact with the RGB channel, and the alpha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:53.520
channel for both the source and destination.&nbsp;
Keep in mind you can't specify a function for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:59.840
each RGB value, only for all of them together.
At this point we just have to enable blending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:06.480
using glEnable(GL_BLEND) right before our windows,&nbsp;
and then disable it right after we are done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:12.480
drawing them so that we don't accidentally affect&nbsp;
anything else. You should always do this with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:18.640
transparent objects. After that just compile and&nbsp;
you should see that the windows are transparent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:26.160
But there is one problem... the blending is all&nbsp;
messed up. It just doesn't look right. And for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:32.800
that we have our friend the Depth Buffer to blame.&nbsp;
Since the windows are drawn in a random order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:39.760
windows that are drawn behind already existing&nbsp;
ones don't get drawn at all since the depth test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:45.200
fails. So in order to draw all the windows&nbsp;
we should draw the furthest one first, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:51.520
the closest one last. Or you could of course be&nbsp;
lazy about it and simply disable the depth buffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:57.840
when drawing the windows, but that will not work&nbsp;
in most circumstances, and I would not recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.840 --> 00:22:03.200
it usually. Another option would be to sort the&nbsp;
windows by their distance from the camera.
&nbsp;

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:09.840
Now sorting has nothing to do with OpenGL, so&nbsp;
I won't be explaining this part, and I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:15.280
encourage you to come up with your own solution&nbsp;
for sorting the windows. The important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:20.960
is that I calculate all the distances from one&nbsp;
window to the camera by subtracting the window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:27.360
position from the camera position and getting the&nbsp;
length of that vector. Then based on that length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:33.520
I sort all my windows from the furthest to the&nbsp;
closest and draw them. It's that simple! You just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:39.200
have to be a bit creative with the sorting part&nbsp;
and how you store your objects. Just keep in mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:45.760
that this method where you just draw the objects&nbsp;
in order is not guaranteed to work 100% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:52.400
time. If different transparent objects intersect&nbsp;
or do some weird stuff, you will get weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:57.680
results. But other methods are pretty complicated&nbsp;
so for now this will have to do.
&nbsp;

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:08.880
In this tutorial I'll show you how to implement&nbsp;
a custom framebuffer into your OpenGL application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:13.360
and how you can use the framebuffer&nbsp;
to achieve post processing effects.
&nbsp;

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:18.160
So first of all, what is a framebuffer?&nbsp;
Well you can think of a framebuffer as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:23.600
collection of multiple buffers that result&nbsp;
in the final image you see on your screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:29.920
So it contains a color buffer, a depth buffer, and&nbsp;
a stencil buffer. Now why would you want to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:35.440
one? Well if we create our own framebuffer,&nbsp;
then we can display it on a rectangle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:40.880
covers the whole screen and then using shaders&nbsp;
modify the pixels displayed on the rectangle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:46.160
to achieve different effects. This is called&nbsp;
post-processing because you process the pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:52.000
after all the rendering has already been done.
Ok, now let's implement the framebuffer. Just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:58.960
any OpenGL object, we create an unsigned int, we&nbsp;
generate the framebuffer using glGenFramebuffers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:04.160
and we bind it. That was it for the framebuffer.&nbsp;
Now we need to add a color texture for it to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:10.160
of any use. So we'll create a texture just like&nbsp;
in the textures tutorial, making sure we clamp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:16.320
the texture to the edges since otherwise certain&nbsp;
effects will bleed from one side of the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:21.760
to the other due to the default repetition of the&nbsp;
texture. Then we simply attach the texture to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:28.240
framebuffer using glFramebufferTexture2D.
Keep in mind we store our color in a texture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:33.840
therefore we can access it from a shader, which we&nbsp;
want to do. But in the case of the depth buffer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:39.440
we don't really care about reading it in a shader&nbsp;
for this tutorial, so instead of using a texture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:44.880
we can use a Render Buffer Object which&nbsp;
is much faster but has the disadvantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:50.480
that you can't read it directly in a shader. We&nbsp;
create it using glGenRenderbuffers, and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:57.760
configure its storage using glRenderbufferStorage,&nbsp;
plugging in GL_RENDERBUFFER, GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:04.720
and the width and height. We use&nbsp;
GL_DEPTH24_STENCIL8 so that we can store in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:11.280
both the stencil buffer and depth buffer. Also be&nbsp;
very careful that all the framebuffers components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:16.960
have the same width and height, otherwise you&nbsp;
might get an error. Then we just attach it to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:22.240
framebuffer using glFramebufferRenderbuffer.
And for a bit of error checking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:28.320
just write this. Sadly the errors are not very&nbsp;
specific, they just give you a number. Here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:33.200
the meanings of the errors you can get.
Now that we have the framebuffer object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:38.560
we can make our rectangle which will always&nbsp;
cover the whole screen, and so we don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:44.800
apply any sort of transformations to it. Now let's&nbsp;
create two very basic shaders for the framebuffer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:49.280
make them into a shader program, and&nbsp;
then send the unit of our texture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:55.440
0 since it's the only texture in this shader.
Now let's handle the drawing part. First we make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.440 --> 00:26:00.480
sure to bind the framebuffer before we draw&nbsp;
anything, including the background. Make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:05.680
sure your buffers are cleared and that you&nbsp;
have depth testing enabled after that. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:10.720
after we are done drawing everything in the scene,&nbsp;
we want to switch back to our default framebuffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:16.320
by binding 0, and draw the rectangle which&nbsp;
displays the framebuffer we've just unbinded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:21.520
Just make sure to disable depth testing so that&nbsp;
the rectangle doesn't fail the depth test.
&nbsp;

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:26.240
Now if you run the program you should be seeing&nbsp;
whatever you were seeing before without any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:31.600
difference. If your screen is a flat color, then&nbsp;
first check that you don't have any errors in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:38.480
your cmd-like window, then make sure your face&nbsp;
culling isn't getting rid of the rectangle (so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:44.000
either change the order of the vertices, or&nbsp;
disable culling when drawing the rectangle).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:48.320
For any other errors check the source code&nbsp;
in the description and look through your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:53.280
code very carefully, there are a lot of&nbsp;
things that can go wrong in this case.
&nbsp;

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:58.000
Ok, now let's make this interesting. In&nbsp;
the framebuffer fragment shader we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:03.280
all kinds of interesting effects. Some easy&nbsp;
ones would be inversing the colors like so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:09.200
or making the image black and white. But these&nbsp;
are not that interesting. To get more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:15.280
results, we want to sample multiple pixels when&nbsp;
we choose the color of a single pixel. For that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:21.120
we first want to declare an array of vec2s which&nbsp;
will represent the offset from the pixel we are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:28.400
to its 8 neighbours. Notice how I divide 1 by 800&nbsp;
to get the width and height of one pixel for my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:34.960
800 by 800 window. After that we want to create a&nbsp;
float array which will represent something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:41.360
a kernel. It's basically a matrix that helps&nbsp;
us achieve cool effects by sort of defining how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:47.600
important each pixel is in relation to the pixel&nbsp;
we are currently on, that being the one in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:53.280
middle. Generally speaking you probably want&nbsp;
them to be somewhat symmetrical and always add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.280 --> 00:28:00.160
up to 1. If they add up to more than 1 the final&nbsp;
color will be brighter, and if they are under 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:06.240
the final color will be darker. Here though they&nbsp;
add up to 0, but that is intentional since this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:12.400
is an edge detection kernel and I want everything&nbsp;
to be really dark, except the edges of things.
&nbsp;

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:18.800
Now all we have to do is to multiply each part of&nbsp;
the kernel with each pixel at a certain offset and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:24.240
add up all of them in this vec3 named color.&nbsp;
Just keep in mind that even though these look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:30.560
like matrices, we don't actually multiply them the&nbsp;
same way we do matrices. Then the last step is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:36.720
output color as the FragColor, and we're done!&nbsp;
I think this kernel gives a pretty cool effect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:41.120
Here are some other kernels you might want to&nbsp;
try out. I suggest you just play around with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:45.920
this a bit and make your own kernels so that&nbsp;
you get a feeling as to how they work.
&nbsp;

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:54.800
In this tutorial I'll show you&nbsp;
what cubemaps are in OpenGL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.800 --> 00:29:00.320
and how you can use them to create skyboxes.
So what are cubemaps? Well they are simply another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:07.120
type of texture, which holds 6 2D textures, one&nbsp;
for each side of a cube. When sampling a cubemap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:12.880
you specify a 3D vector instead of a 2D one.&nbsp;
This allows you to easily sample between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:18.480
all 6 sides of the cube. And since the coordinates&nbsp;
of the cube correspond to the sampling vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:25.360
there is no need for UVs. The most common uses for&nbsp;
cubemaps are quadsphere texturing and skyboxes.
&nbsp;

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:30.320
So now let's code it in. The first thing you&nbsp;
need to do is to write out the cube vertices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:37.200
and indices. Then we'll want to create a VAO,&nbsp;
VBO, and EBO just like in the first tutorials.
&nbsp;

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:43.040
Now let's create an array with 6 strings that will&nbsp;
hold the paths to the 6 images we'll be using for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:48.880
the skybox. Then we want to create the cubemap&nbsp;
texture itself just like any other texture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:55.040
except that where we put GL_TEXTURE_2D&nbsp;
before, we not put GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.040 --> 00:30:00.560
Make sure to clamp the texture in all three&nbsp;
directions since the texture is a cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:05.520
therefore 3D. This clamping should&nbsp;
prevent any seams from showing up.
&nbsp;

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:11.760
And now we'll go over all six textures and read&nbsp;
them using the stb library, putting them in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:17.200
cube texture once we read them. Notice how I&nbsp;
disable the vertical flipping. This is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:22.960
unlike most textures in OpenGL, cubemaps are&nbsp;
expected to start in the top left corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:29.360
not the bottom left corner. Also notice how&nbsp;
I add i to GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP_POSITIVE_X.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:34.640
This represents the side of the cube&nbsp;
I am currently assigning a texture to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:41.120
and I am adding i to it in order to cycle through&nbsp;
all the sides. Here is the order of the sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:47.600
which you can find in the OpenGL docs, and here is&nbsp;
the order we wrote the paths in. Notice something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:55.840
weird? Well, normally in OpenGL, the front is&nbsp;
in the negative Z direction, but for cubemaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.840 --> 00:31:02.800
the front is in the positive Z direction. That&nbsp;
means that cubemaps work in a left-handed system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:09.520
while most of OpenGL works in a right-handed&nbsp;
system. This can be very confusing, and I honestly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:15.600
have no idea why they chose to do this, but oh&nbsp;
well. Keep in mind you will likely get small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:22.160
bugs because of this if you are not careful. In my&nbsp;
case, my right texture kept being displayed upside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:29.760
down for some reason. To fix that I just flipped&nbsp;
the texture in an image editor. So be prepared for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:35.120
this sort of stuff since from what I've heard&nbsp;
it can happen pretty often with skyboxes.
&nbsp;

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:41.040
Ok, back to the tutorial. We'll now need to&nbsp;
create two shaders for the skybox. The vertex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:46.960
shader will take in the coordinates, output&nbsp;
texture coordinates, and also take in uniforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:52.800
for matrix transformations. In the main function&nbsp;
create a vec4 which holds the final transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:58.800
coordinates. Now since these coordinates are now&nbsp;
in screen space, we'll do something a bit weird.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:04.880
Instead of feeding gl_Position the coordinates&nbsp;
as they are, we'll give it the x, y, w,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:10.960
and again w components. This will result&nbsp;
in the z component always being 1 after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:16.960
the perspective division. And since the depth&nbsp;
buffer takes the z component as its depth value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:23.360
the skybox will always have a depth value of&nbsp;
1, so the furthest depth value possible, thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:29.440
being behind all objects, as it should be. And&nbsp;
finally we want to export the texture coordinates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:35.520
as the positions, except we'll flip the z axis&nbsp;
to combat the coordinate system change.
&nbsp;

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:41.280
For the fragment shader we just want to import the&nbsp;
texture coordinates, the cubemap, and then set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:46.000
fragColor to equal the texture. That's it.
Back in the main function I'll create a shader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:51.680
program and export the skybox texture unit.&nbsp;
All that's left now is the drawing part.
&nbsp;

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:57.680
We'll start by setting the depth function&nbsp;
to GL_LEQUAL instead of the default GL_LESS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:04.800
since our skybox is right on the edge, aka 1, so&nbsp;
we need that equal sign. Then we'll activate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:10.240
shader and create the view and projection matrices&nbsp;
which are identical to the ones we created in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:18.160
camera class, except for one small detail. For&nbsp;
the view matrix, we downgrade it to a mat3, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:24.960
then we scale it back up to a mat4. This will make&nbsp;
the last row and column of the matrix equal to 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:30.080
thus having no effect on translations.&nbsp;
We only want the skybox to rotate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:34.640
not move around. Then simply export&nbsp;
these matrices to the vertex shader.
&nbsp;

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:40.320
Now the final part is to draw the cubemap itself&nbsp;
just like any other object, except that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.320 --> 00:33:46.960
we bind the texture, we use GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP&nbsp;
instead of the usual GL_TEXTURE_2D. Don't forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:52.800
to switch the depth testing to it's default&nbsp;
GL_LESS after you are done with the skybox.
&nbsp;

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:58.720
If you boot up your program, you should now have&nbsp;
a nice skybox all around you. If any of the faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:04.880
are inverted or something like that, play around&nbsp;
with the orientation of your images in an image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.880 --> 00:34:10.880
editor. Trust me, it's a lot easier to just do&nbsp;
that rather than trying to find a little logic bug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:16.640
caused by the two different coordinate systems,&nbsp;
and the different texture reading origins. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:22.480
have seams that are clearly lines, then you can&nbsp;
try adding glEnable(GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP_SEAMLESS)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:29.120
somewhere in your code. If your seams are&nbsp;
not lines, but simply clear differences in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:34.320
color between one face and another, then you&nbsp;
probably just have a shitty skybox. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:41.120
can't see anything or only parts of the skybox,&nbsp;
then you probably have back face culling enabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:46.080
and wrote the indices in the wrong&nbsp;
winding order. Simply disable face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:51.200
culling when drawing the skybox, or write the&nbsp;
indices in the correct winding order.
&nbsp;

00:34:56.480 --> 00:35:00.720
In this tutorial I will show you what the&nbsp;
geometry shader is and how you can use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:06.480
to create things such as visible normals.
So far we've only used the vertex shader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:13.360
and fragment shader, which suffice in most&nbsp;
situations. But sometimes between the vertex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:19.360
and fragment shaders you want to have an extra&nbsp;
step to modify the geometry of your meshes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:25.040
Even though it might seem like you can do that&nbsp;
in the vertex shader, you can only do things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:31.840
individual vertices. If you wanted to modify a&nbsp;
whole triangle, so a group of vertices, then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:37.600
would need to use the geometry shader. The second&nbsp;
advantage of the geometry shader is that it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:44.080
switch between different types of primitives,&nbsp;
and so can create or delete vertices.
&nbsp;

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:50.800
So let's begin by adding the geometry shader to&nbsp;
our shader class. Notice how I am doing the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:56.720
same thing as for the other two shaders,&nbsp;
except that I use GL_GEOMETRY_SHADER. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.720 --> 00:36:03.040
that we support custom geometry shaders, let's&nbsp;
create a geometry shader that does nothing.
&nbsp;

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:09.520
Just like any other shader we'll begin with the&nbsp;
version. Then we need two layouts written like so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:15.040
The first layout signifies what type of primitive&nbsp;
we receive, which can be one of the following,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:20.080
while the second layout shows what&nbsp;
type of primitive we are outputting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:24.880
which can be one of the following. In&nbsp;
this case we want to receive a triangle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:31.520
and export a triangle. Then we have our outputs to&nbsp;
the fragment shader. Keep in mind you should pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:37.120
data from the vertex shader to the geometry&nbsp;
shader and then to the fragment shader.
&nbsp;

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:42.960
Now for importing data into the geometry shader,&nbsp;
we need to do something a bit different. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:49.760
of simply having an "in", we'll have a sort of C&nbsp;
structure written out like so. Note that we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:55.280
have to include the position in this because&nbsp;
it is already built into a default structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.280 --> 00:37:01.200
just like this one called gl_in. Now we need to&nbsp;
go back to the vertex shader and replace all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:08.080
outgoing data with the exact same structure except&nbsp;
for this last part and "out" instead of "in".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:14.160
Make sure that everything else from the structure&nbsp;
is identical to its counterpart in the geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:20.400
shader. Notice how I also included the projection&nbsp;
matrix. That is because we only want to apply the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:27.520
projection matrix AFTER we modify our geometry.&nbsp;
Now to assign data to these outgoing values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:32.880
we simply write the name we gave them plus a dot&nbsp;
and the name of the variable we want to assign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:40.320
data to. Very similar to a C or C++ structure.
Now in the geometry shader we have all the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:44.480
we need, so all that's left to do&nbsp;
is to assemble this data together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:51.360
To do that we simply assign the position, normal,&nbsp;
color, and texture coordinates their data. Notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:57.920
how here I also have an index besides the name of&nbsp;
the part of the structure I want to access. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:03.520
because we are in the geometry shader and thus&nbsp;
we essentially have an array of such structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:09.200
each with different values for a specific vertex.&nbsp;
Once we are done assigning the values of a vertex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:14.640
we must use EmitVertex() to declare that we&nbsp;
are done with this vertex, VertEx veRTeX veRtEx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:19.680
vERTeX. Now we can do the same thing for the other&nbsp;
two vertices, and once we are done with all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:26.160
vertices we need for a triangle, we declare that&nbsp;
our primitive is complete using EndPrimitive().&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:30.400
And that was it for the default geometry&nbsp;
shader. If you run your program you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:36.080
have exactly what you had before, amazing!
But that's really boring, so let's change things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:41.840
up a bit and make the geometry shader more fun.&nbsp;
Something that is extremely easy to do is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:48.240
calculate the surface normal using a cross product&nbsp;
and then add that normal to the three positions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:54.320
the primitive. This will make your meshes look as&nbsp;
if all the primitives exploded or glitched out.
&nbsp;

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:59.440
Now let's do something a bit more serious.&nbsp;
Let's create a new shader which will take our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:05.440
default vertex shader, a new fragment shader,&nbsp;
and a new geometry shader. And let's draw our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:11.280
model using this shader after we draw it using the&nbsp;
default shader. Now for the fragment shader we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:18.160
simply output a flat color, nothing fancy.
But for the geometry shader, we'll get a triangle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:26.720
and output not a triangle, but multiple lines!&nbsp;
So now we are actually destroying and creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:34.480
new geometry since as you can see we'll output 6&nbsp;
vertices even though we only got 3. Then we'll use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:40.160
the exact same structure, and in the main function&nbsp;
we'll simply retrieve the first vertex position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:45.680
and declare it. But then instead of moving to&nbsp;
the next vertex, we'll remain on this one but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:51.680
add to it the normal of the vertex and then emit&nbsp;
it. This essentially creates a line across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:57.600
normal of the vertex. Now we want to do the same&nbsp;
for the other vertices, but in order to not link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:03.360
all the lines together we must first end this&nbsp;
line by using EndPrimitive and then move on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:07.840
After doing this for all the vertices we&nbsp;
can press play and see that we are now able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:13.840
to visualize all the normals of our model which&nbsp;
can be pretty useful for debugging purposes.
&nbsp;

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:18.560
Keep in mind that geometry shaders can&nbsp;
also be very useful for things such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:23.840
grass or subdividing models. So they&nbsp;
are actually pretty interesting.
&nbsp;

00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:33.760
In this tutorial I'll show you what Instancing is&nbsp;
and how you can use it to massively improve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:40.640
performance and looks of your OpenGL project.
So instancing is simply a feature that allows you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:46.640
to draw a mesh multiple times in a single&nbsp;
draw call. Now why would you want this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:52.320
Well consider these scenarios where I have a belt&nbsp;
of asteroids all made out of a single asteroid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:58.800
mesh that is deformed in the vertex shader to give&nbsp;
some variety. In the scenario on the left I simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:05.200
have a loop that draws each asteroid individually.&nbsp;
So that means that each asteroid has a draw call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:10.800
Now in the scenario on the right I draw all the&nbsp;
asteroids together. So that means that I only have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:16.400
a single draw call. If you look at the performance&nbsp;
difference, you'll see it is massive.
&nbsp;

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:21.440
Now let's code it in. I'll start with the&nbsp;
code already written for the first scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:28.480
since it's nothing new for this series. So in&nbsp;
order to enable instacing, all we have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:35.280
is to use glDrawElementsInstanced instead of&nbsp;
glDrawElements, and add at the end of it how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:41.280
many instances of the mesh we want. The only&nbsp;
problem is that this will draw all the meshes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.280 --> 00:41:47.520
in the exact same positions, so it is useless.
There are multiple ways you could move each mesh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:53.680
to a unique position though. You could have code&nbsp;
that does that in the vertex shader for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:58.560
Using gl_InstanceID you'll get the index of&nbsp;
the the instance you are currently drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:04.000
and so you can use that for controlled random&nbsp;
number generation. Alternatively you could have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:10.160
uniform with all the transformations and retrieve&nbsp;
the correct transformation for a specific instance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:16.800
using gl_InstanceID. But the problem with this&nbsp;
is that uniforms can't store that much data. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:22.640
the best way to have a lot of transformations and&nbsp;
not have the generation inside the vertex shader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:28.960
is to store the transformations inside a vertex&nbsp;
buffer that is attached to the VAO of the mesh.
&nbsp;

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:35.040
So let's start by creating a VBO constructor&nbsp;
which takes a vector of mat4s. Then we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:40.720
to add an unsigned integer public variable to&nbsp;
the Mesh class that will signify the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:47.040
instances we desire. And of course we also need&nbsp;
to add it to the constructor to easily change it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:53.280
Also in the constructor we should add the vector&nbsp;
of matrix transformations for the instances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:59.280
so that we can plug it into the vertex buffer.&nbsp;
Now in the Mesh.cpp file we want to create a VBO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:06.800
for the instances and then link its attributes to&nbsp;
the VAO only if we are drawing more than one mesh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:13.440
Make sure to link the matrix as 4 different vec4s&nbsp;
since otherwise your program won't work. And at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:21.600
the end use glVertexAttribDivisor plugging in the&nbsp;
layout number of each vec4 and 1. This 1 means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:28.160
that the vec4 will be used for a whole instance.&nbsp;
If it were 0, then the vec4 would be used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.160 --> 00:43:34.560
a vertex, and then the next vec4 will be used&nbsp;
for the next vertex, which we for sure don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:40.400
want. And just to make it clear, if it were&nbsp;
2 it would be used for two instances before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:45.920
switch to the next vec4. Now let's also&nbsp;
limit the use of glDrawElementsInstanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:49.840
only for multiple instances.
For the Model class we need to do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:55.440
exact same thing as for the Mesh class where&nbsp;
we add instanceMatrix to the constructor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.440 --> 00:44:00.880
as a variable, and instancing as a&nbsp;
variable yet again. Now in our loadMesh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:06.640
function we want to include the instancing and&nbsp;
instanceMatrix in the Mesh creation process.
&nbsp;

00:44:06.640 --> 00:44:11.760
Now we need a slightly modified shader&nbsp;
for instance drawing to accommodate for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:17.680
the new layout. So create a new shader program&nbsp;
specifically for the asteroids using a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.680 --> 00:44:23.360
vertex shader and the default fragment shader.&nbsp;
The vertex shader will be identical to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:29.920
default one except we'll have a 5th layout for the&nbsp;
instanceMatrix, we'll delete the uniforms we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.920 --> 00:44:36.000
need, and replace all transformations with just&nbsp;
the instanceMatrix one. Don't forget to export the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:41.440
lighting uniforms in the main function as well.
Then all that's left to do is to merge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:48.080
translation, rotation, and scale matrices together&nbsp;
and add all of them to the instanceMatrix vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:53.760
Then simply add that and the instancing number&nbsp;
to the Model constructor of the asteroids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.760 --> 00:45:00.160
and draw the asteroids once. Remember that&nbsp;
we only need to call the draw function once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:07.360
Now compile and witness thousands upon thousands&nbsp;
of asteroids with a minimal performance impact. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:12.080
course keep in mind that the performance&nbsp;
will differ from one GPU to another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:17.840
so it might not work as well on yours&nbsp;
as it would on someone else's.
&nbsp;

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:25.360
In this tutorial I'll show you&nbsp;
what Anti-Aliasing is and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:31.360
you can implement it in your OpenGL project.
So you might have noticed that while horizontal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:38.240
and vertical edges look extremely crisp,&nbsp;
diagonal edges tend to look a bit choppy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:44.480
like a flight of stairs. This is due to how&nbsp;
we display images on our screens. Since our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:51.200
displays are made out of a bunch of tiny squares,&nbsp;
aka pixels, it is impossible to have a smooth line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:56.800
on the diagonal. But thankfully, we can&nbsp;
fake smoothness by bleeding the color of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:03.520
an edge into the adjacent pixels like so.
Now, these jagged edges are called Aliasing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:09.920
and an Anti-Aliasing technique is what helps us&nbsp;
get better edges. There are multiple techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.920 --> 00:46:17.120
for Anti-Aliasing each with their advantages&nbsp;
and disadvantages, but today I'll focus on MSAA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:23.840
which stands for Multi Sampling Anti-Aliasing.&nbsp;
So what does this Multi Sampling refer too?
&nbsp;

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:30.080
Well in the rasterization part of the pipeline&nbsp;
primitives are filled in. The way it is decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:35.840
which pixels should be given a color and which&nbsp;
should not, is by checking if the sample point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:42.240
of a pixel, which is normally in the center of it,&nbsp;
is inside the shape of the primitive. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.240 --> 00:46:48.320
that if the sample point is even just slightly&nbsp;
outside the triangle, it won't get sampled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:55.040
even though you would think it should at least do&nbsp;
so partially. Well, that is where MSAA comes in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.040 --> 00:46:59.440
As you might have guessed, this technique&nbsp;
simply adds multiple sampling points so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:05.360
that a more accurate result can be reached.
Here for example, two out of the 4 sampling points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:11.440
are inside the triangle, and so the color of that&nbsp;
pixel will be somewhere between the color of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.440 --> 00:47:17.280
background and the color of the primitive.
Now let's actually implement this. Let's start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:23.440
off by creating a variable where we specify how&nbsp;
many samples we desire. Now, if you don't have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:30.160
framebuffer, then you can just give a window hint&nbsp;
to GLFW saying you want GLFW_SAMPLES and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:36.320
number of samples you want, and then activate&nbsp;
GL_MULTISAMPLE. That was it for this tutorial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:42.480
as... nah I'm joking. But for real though, if you&nbsp;
don't have a framebuffer that's all you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.480 --> 00:47:48.720
do, you are done. If you do have a framebuffer,&nbsp;
then you'll want to delete the GLFW part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:53.920
Instead you need to go to your framebuffer&nbsp;
and replace all GL_TEXTURE_2D with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.920 --> 00:48:01.440
GL_TEXTURE_2D_MULTISAMPLE. Then replace&nbsp;
glTexImage2D with glTexImage2DMultisamples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:06.960
plugging in the type of texture, the number of&nbsp;
samples, the color format, the width, the height,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:12.000
and whether or not you want all samples to be&nbsp;
in the exact same position in the pixels.
&nbsp;

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:16.960
Then for the renderbuffer object we need&nbsp;
to change from glRenderbufferStorage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:22.400
to glRenderbufferStorageMultismaple and&nbsp;
add the number of samples we want.
&nbsp;

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:27.920
Now the problem is that we can't do any sort&nbsp;
of post-processing on this framebuffer anymore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:33.680
since it has multisampling enabled. So to get&nbsp;
around that we'll need a normal framebuffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:38.400
which we can post-process. This is just like&nbsp;
the one I made in the framebuffer tutorial.
&nbsp;

00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:43.920
Now in the main function we want to make&nbsp;
sure we first bind the multisampling FBO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:49.840
clear the screen, clear the buffers, and enable&nbsp;
depth testing. Then we draw everything we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:56.960
to draw. After that we bind the multisampling&nbsp;
FBO as read only, and the postprocessing FBO as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:02.400
draw only. Now using glBlitFramebuffer&nbsp;
we'll resolve all the multisampling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:08.400
copy the result onto the post-processing FBO.
Now make sure you bind the default framebuffer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:13.920
and draw the framebuffer rectangle using the&nbsp;
postprocessing texture. Run the program and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:19.520
you'll see that the edges of primitives are&nbsp;
a lot smoother and nicer. Just be aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:26.160
applying kernels in the post processing will&nbsp;
essentially overwrite the anti aliasing and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:31.120
you may end up with aliasing again. As&nbsp;
for the number of samples you should use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:38.720
I suggest using either 2, 4, or 8. You can&nbsp;
go up to 16 and 32 on some GPUs but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:45.520
improvement/performance ratio is not worth it.
That was it for this tutorial, as always the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:49.840
source code and all resources used&nbsp;
are in the description. Bye!

